(typeof module==="object"&&module.exports?require("../curriculum.js"):BodyguardCurriculum).register(1,[["q1-c4ef0be43a2c","s1.0-a6a5b795304c","1.0","Introduction","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nscience that studies the form and composition of the body’s structures","anatomy",["physiology","pressure","homeostasis"],"anatomy: science that studies the form and composition of the body’s structures.","The answer begins with A and has 1 word.","fig-ch01_00_01","f-a89e79c0bfd9"],["q1-58a8e1035168","s1.0-31c1f7fd0552","1.0","Introduction","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nsteady state of body systems that living organisms maintain","homeostasis",["physiology","pressure","anatomy"],"homeostasis: steady state of body systems that living organisms maintain.","The answer begins with H and has 1 word.","eip-id1169806326587","f-41c6f6a0a3d2"],["q1-8dc4fd3c1609","s1.0-ac014429ec9c","1.0","Introduction","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nscience that studies the chemistry, biochemistry, and physics of the body’s functions","physiology",["homeostasis","anatomy","pressure"],"physiology: science that studies the chemistry, biochemistry, and physics of the body’s functions.","The answer begins with P and has 1 word.","fig-ch01_00_01","f-a5debefa7e54"],["q1-ebf8b8dbc874","s1.0-3b4c0c6565e0","1.0","Introduction","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nforce exerted by a substance in contact with another substance","pressure",["physiology","anatomy","homeostasis"],"pressure: force exerted by a substance in contact with another substance.","The answer begins with P and has 1 word.","fig-ch01_00_01","f-e777c9383bbe"],["q1-8ebeca18e326","s1.1-5f435eb30281","1.1","Overview of Anatomy and Physiology","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nsmallest independently functioning unit of all organisms; in animals, a cell contains cytoplasm, composed of fluid and organelles","cell",["homeostasis","anatomy","physiology"],"cell: smallest independently functioning unit of all organisms; in animals, a cell contains cytoplasm, composed of fluid and organelles.","The answer begins with C and has 1 word.","fs-id2080383","f-6ec30d3c75f9"],["q1-77f960f5dcdb","s1.1-66cebff1d710","1.1","Overview of Anatomy and Physiology","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nstudy of the larger structures of the body, typically with the unaided eye; also referred to macroscopic anatomy","gross anatomy",["systemic anatomy","microscopic anatomy","regional anatomy"],"gross anatomy: study of the larger structures of the body, typically with the unaided eye; also referred to macroscopic anatomy.","The answer begins with G and has 2 words.","fs-id2608267","f-091f1f7a5bf3"],["q1-687fe9a54bbe","s1.1-45f96647edd4","1.1","Overview of Anatomy and Physiology","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nstudy of very small structures of the body using magnification","microscopic anatomy",["systemic anatomy","regional anatomy","gross anatomy"],"microscopic anatomy: study of very small structures of the body using magnification.","The answer begins with M and has 2 words.","fs-id2608267","f-33e05feceabb"],["q1-b53715f80b26","s1.1-1c5ce0dbf9bf","1.1","Overview of Anatomy and Physiology","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nfunctionally distinct structure composed of two or more types of tissues","organ",["anatomy","physiology","homeostasis"],"organ: functionally distinct structure composed of two or more types of tissues.","The answer begins with O and has 1 word.","fs-id2297149","f-875ccf6a3864"],["q1-54eed2be46a2","s1.1-aba4132ee4d7","1.1","Overview of Anatomy and Physiology","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nstudy of the structures that contribute to specific body regions","regional anatomy",["gross anatomy","systemic anatomy","microscopic anatomy"],"regional anatomy: study of the structures that contribute to specific body regions.","The answer begins with R and has 2 words.","fs-id1636111","f-9ad3898aee5a"],["q1-2939aeceeb69","s1.1-b69eb07f8d70","1.1","Overview of Anatomy and Physiology","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nstudy of the structures that contribute to specific body systems","systemic anatomy",["regional anatomy","gross anatomy","microscopic anatomy"],"systemic anatomy: study of the structures that contribute to specific body systems.","The answer begins with S and has 2 words.","fs-id1636111","f-bdda0e49a2ea"],["q1-1c2302a26f92","s1.1-a6a5b795304c","1.1","Overview of Anatomy and Physiology","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nHuman ____ is the scientific study of the body’s structures.","anatomy",["physiology","homeostasis","effector"],"Human anatomy is the scientific study of the body’s structures.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2264559","f-d70fef78d81e"],["q1-8dae9312b5a0","s1.1-a6a5b795304c","1.1","Overview of Anatomy and Physiology","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nDissection is still used in medical schools, ____ courses, and in pathology labs.","anatomy",["physiology","homeostasis","cranial"],"Dissection is still used in medical schools, anatomy courses, and in pathology labs.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2264559","f-90d45643eeda"],["q1-3a3a2bd1ce45","s1.1-a6a5b795304c","1.1","Overview of Anatomy and Physiology","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nLike most scientific disciplines, ____ has areas of specialization.","anatomy",["homeostasis","physiology","effector"],"Like most scientific disciplines, anatomy has areas of specialization.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2608267","f-b730b7a1d123"],["q1-270a2793c8d6","s1.1-45f96647edd4","1.1","Overview of Anatomy and Physiology","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ includes cytology, the study of cells and histology, the study of tissues.","Microscopic anatomy",["gross anatomy","regional anatomy","systemic anatomy"],"Microscopic anatomy includes cytology, the study of cells and histology, the study of tissues.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2608267","f-11bb02ffa7bf"],["q1-189907aa1833","s1.1-b69eb07f8d70","1.1","Overview of Anatomy and Physiology","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ is the study of the structures that make up a discrete body system—that is, a group of structures that work together to perform a unique body function.","systemic anatomy",["microscopic anatomy","gross anatomy","regional anatomy"],"In contrast, systemic anatomy is the study of the structures that make up a discrete body system—that is, a group of structures that work together to perform a unique body function.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1636111","f-e891a5e1c096"],["q1-a4b3077459f1","s1.1-ac014429ec9c","1.1","Overview of Anatomy and Physiology","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nWhereas anatomy is about structure, ____ is about function.","physiology",["homeostasis","regional anatomy","microscopic anatomy"],"Whereas anatomy is about structure, physiology is about function.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1707081","f-6a518fde8783"],["q1-b1ec4507dce5","s1.1-31c1f7fd0552","1.1","Overview of Anatomy and Physiology","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nMuch of the study of physiology centers on the body’s tendency toward ____.","homeostasis",["anatomy","gross anatomy","regional anatomy"],"Much of the study of physiology centers on the body’s tendency toward homeostasis.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1707081","f-5f15f63cc035"],["q1-3ee5ba5ac081","s1.1-31c1f7fd0552","1.1","Overview of Anatomy and Physiology","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ is the state of steady internal conditions maintained by living things.","Homeostasis",["physiology","anatomy","microscopic anatomy"],"Homeostasis is the state of steady internal conditions maintained by living things.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1707081","f-dec468825969"],["q1-5ed3a2be7bb8","s1.1-ac014429ec9c","1.1","Overview of Anatomy and Physiology","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe study of ____ certainly includes observation, both with the naked eye and with microscopes, as well as manipulations and measurements.","physiology",["homeostasis","anatomy","systemic anatomy"],"The study of physiology certainly includes observation, both with the naked eye and with microscopes, as well as manipulations and measurements.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1707081","f-b81f69c2b10c"],["q1-aea0aab86e81","s1.1-ac014429ec9c","1.1","Overview of Anatomy and Physiology","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nCurrent advances in ____ usually depend on carefully designed laboratory experiments that reveal the functions of the many structures and chemical compounds that make up the human body.","physiology",["anatomy","homeostasis","microscopic anatomy"],"However, current advances in physiology usually depend on carefully designed laboratory experiments that reveal the functions of the many structures and chemical compounds that make up the human body.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1707081","f-490a40658f4f"],["q1-ced3fdf3ade9","s1.1-ac014429ec9c","1.1","Overview of Anatomy and Physiology","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nLike anatomists, physiologists typically specialize in a particular branch of ____.","physiology",["anatomy","homeostasis","gross anatomy"],"Like anatomists, physiologists typically specialize in a particular branch of physiology.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2297149","f-05198d02f8b0"],["q1-609f0534a98b","s1.2-6a3c615ba762","1.2","The Levels of Organization","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ngroup of organs that work together to carry out a particular function","organ system",["physiology","anatomy","normal range"],"organ system: group of organs that work together to carry out a particular function.","The answer begins with O and has 2 words.","fs-id1641187","f-dfe83ad7ad69"],["q1-f2dc36026dd7","s1.2-454a46df2e65","1.2","Structural Organization of the Human Body","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nliving being that has a cellular structure and that can independently perform all physiologic functions necessary for life","organism",["anatomy","physiology","organ system"],"organism: living being that has a cellular structure and that can independently perform all physiologic functions necessary for life.","The answer begins with O and has 1 word.","fig-ch01_02_01","f-551eece274f2"],["q1-b0f4684321a3","s1.2-bb8eb17cab02","1.2","The Levels of Organization","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ngroup of similar or closely related cells that act together to perform a specific function","tissue",["physiology","anatomy","organ system"],"tissue: group of similar or closely related cells that act together to perform a specific function.","The answer begins with T and has 1 word.","fs-id1641187","f-f61f22e95264"],["q1-e0b0e74d54af","s1.2-ac014429ec9c","1.2","The Levels of Organization","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAll living structures of human anatomy contain cells, and almost all functions of human ____ are performed in cells or are initiated by cells.","physiology",["organ system","organelles","superior"],"All living structures of human anatomy contain cells, and almost all functions of human physiology are performed in cells or are initiated by cells.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2348119","f-033bc283273a"],["q1-bff63e7282a8","s1.2-7d7476c8c2a2","1.2","The Levels of Organization","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA human cell typically consists of flexible membranes that enclose cytoplasm, a water-based cellular fluid together with a variety of tiny functioning units called ____.","organelles",["physiology","anatomy","organ system"],"A human cell typically consists of flexible membranes that enclose cytoplasm, a water-based cellular fluid together with a variety of tiny functioning units called organelles.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1641187","f-218e1476cca6"],["q1-f3d362d34f3e","s1.2-6a3c615ba762","1.2","The Levels of Organization","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAn ____ is a group of organs that work together to perform major functions or meet physiological needs of the body.","organ system",["anatomy","physiology","regional anatomy"],"An organ system is a group of organs that work together to perform major functions or meet physiological needs of the body.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1641187","f-b3f8a2eca323"],["q1-ed378843a9ae","s1.3-00bd9ca41c94","1.3","Metabolism","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nassembly of more complex molecules from simpler molecules","anabolism",["physiology","catabolism","differentiation"],"anabolism: assembly of more complex molecules from simpler molecules.","The answer begins with A and has 1 word.","fs-id2514677","f-8ea8c68bcc67"],["q1-482391c5bfc7","s1.3-c417c959756a","1.3","Metabolism","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nbreaking down of more complex molecules into simpler molecules","catabolism",["physiology","metabolism","anabolism"],"catabolism: breaking down of more complex molecules into simpler molecules.","The answer begins with C and has 1 word.","fs-id2514677","f-b1d6050a635c"],["q1-be3cc7a46d16","s1.3-05134426bcd1","1.3","Functions of Human Life","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nchanges an organism goes through during its life","development",["reproduction","physiology","responsiveness"],"development: changes an organism goes through during its life.","The answer begins with D and has 1 word.","fs-id1381566","f-202bf2d38851"],["q1-81a8068eb78b","s1.3-a0416830681c","1.3","Development, growth and reproduction","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nprocess by which unspecialized cells become specialized in structure and function","differentiation",["metabolism","catabolism","anabolism"],"differentiation: process by which unspecialized cells become specialized in structure and function.","The answer begins with D and has 1 word.","fs-id2305104","f-bcb0a9b30c71"],["q1-5aa39c9af5d2","s1.3-34a7962212ab","1.3","Development, growth and reproduction","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nprocess of increasing in size","growth",["responsiveness","differentiation","physiology"],"growth: process of increasing in size.","The answer begins with G and has 1 word.","fs-id2305104","f-cd52a2b64e1b"],["q1-8fac6a86d259","s1.3-671e483513b1","1.3","Functions of Human Life","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nsum of all of the body’s chemical reactions","metabolism",["growth","physiology","reproduction"],"metabolism: sum of all of the body’s chemical reactions.","The answer begins with M and has 1 word.","fs-id1381566","f-446805a3dfb7"],["q1-50f4f49f7de2","s1.3-7ff5735c7072","1.3","Functions of Human Life","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nprocess by which new organisms are generated","reproduction",["catabolism","metabolism","differentiation"],"reproduction: process by which new organisms are generated.","The answer begins with R and has 1 word.","fs-id1381566","f-617bd6847f8c"],["q1-18fe1ee3c09a","s1.3-ec7d19a2ffd5","1.3","Functions of Human Life","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nability of an organisms or a system to adjust to changes in conditions","responsiveness",["physiology","growth","metabolism"],"responsiveness: ability of an organisms or a system to adjust to changes in conditions.","The answer begins with R and has 1 word.","fs-id1381566","f-ef9c6dce2741"],["q1-a38d21b5e31c","s1.3-ac014429ec9c","1.3","Functions of Human Life","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe different organ systems each have different functions and therefore unique roles to perform in ____.","physiology",["metabolism","differentiation","growth"],"The different organ systems each have different functions and therefore unique roles to perform in physiology.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1381566","f-712ecb949973"],["q1-4b10fa2235d4","s1.3-00bd9ca41c94","1.3","Metabolism","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ is the process whereby smaller, simpler molecules are combined into larger, more complex substances.","Anabolism",["physiology","responsiveness","growth"],"Anabolism is the process whereby smaller, simpler molecules are combined into larger, more complex substances.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2970538","f-6eca8ea392d3"],["q1-5e16ff8313b2","s1.3-c417c959756a","1.3","Metabolism","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ is the process by which larger more complex substances are broken down into smaller simpler molecules.","Catabolism",["reproduction","anabolism","responsiveness"],"Catabolism is the process by which larger more complex substances are broken down into smaller simpler molecules.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2970538","f-2beac68a9144"],["q1-4c062d0959c0","s1.3-671e483513b1","1.3","Metabolism","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ is the sum of all anabolic and catabolic reactions that take place in the body.","Metabolism",["reproduction","growth","physiology"],"Metabolism is the sum of all anabolic and catabolic reactions that take place in the body.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1939127","f-04e6584f3c47"],["q1-4c304a62302c","s1.3-c417c959756a","1.3","Metabolism","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nBoth anabolism and ____ occur simultaneously and continuously to keep you alive.","catabolism",["growth","reproduction","metabolism"],"Both anabolism and catabolism occur simultaneously and continuously to keep you alive.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1939127","f-5b1a53fb7dd1"],["q1-9239228a065e","s1.3-060b6d0de851","1.3","Metabolism","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nEvery cell in your body makes use of a chemical compound, ____, to store and release energy.","adenosine triphosphate (ATP)",["differentiation","anabolism","growth"],"Every cell in your body makes use of a chemical compound, adenosine triphosphate (ATP), to store and release energy.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2578934","f-2556930b258c"],["q1-0a684a0bce89","s1.3-00bd9ca41c94","1.3","Metabolism","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe cell stores energy in the synthesis (____) of ATP, then moves the ATP molecules to the location where energy is needed to fuel cellular activities.","anabolism",["growth","responsiveness","metabolism"],"The cell stores energy in the synthesis (anabolism) of ATP, then moves the ATP molecules to the location where energy is needed to fuel cellular activities.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2578934","f-5b8f516d82e6"],["q1-3da787b46c50","s1.3-c417c959756a","1.3","Metabolism","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThen the ATP is broken down (____) and a controlled amount of energy is released, which is used by the cell to perform a particular job.","catabolism",["differentiation","physiology","reproduction"],"Then the ATP is broken down (catabolism) and a controlled amount of energy is released, which is used by the cell to perform a particular job.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2578934","f-adba01cd92d2"],["q1-9dcef744031e","s1.3-ec7d19a2ffd5","1.3","Responsiveness","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAn example of ____ to external stimuli could include moving toward sources of food and water and away from perceived dangers.","responsiveness",["anabolism","differentiation","growth"],"An example of responsiveness to external stimuli could include moving toward sources of food and water and away from perceived dangers.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1896316","f-77fd871f1511"],["q1-6bcd3909fddb","s1.3-a0416830681c","1.3","Development, growth and reproduction","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nDevelopment includes the process of ____, in which unspecialized cells become specialized in structure and function to perform certain tasks in the body.","differentiation",["responsiveness","anabolism","reproduction"],"Development includes the process of differentiation, in which unspecialized cells become specialized in structure and function to perform certain tasks in the body.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2305104","f-f547374859ad"],["q1-b9c16101b691","s1.3-7ff5735c7072","1.3","Development, growth and reproduction","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ is the formation of a new organism from parent organisms.","Reproduction",["differentiation","growth","catabolism"],"Reproduction is the formation of a new organism from parent organisms.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2002399","f-26c74f110507"],["q1-28f5c92485aa","s1.3-7ff5735c7072","1.3","Development, growth and reproduction","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIn humans, ____ is carried out by the male and female reproductive systems.","reproduction",["metabolism","physiology","anabolism"],"In humans, reproduction is carried out by the male and female reproductive systems.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2002399","f-ad20f99f3fa2"],["q1-c1b9526606dc","s1.3-7ff5735c7072","1.3","Development, growth and reproduction","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nBecause death will come to all complex organisms, without ____, the line of organisms would end.","reproduction",["anabolism","responsiveness","metabolism"],"Because death will come to all complex organisms, without reproduction, the line of organisms would end.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2002399","f-24fcd0b4049c"],["q1-cb19594491da","s1.4-3dde59ff3d79","1.4","Decompression Sickness","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndescribes a position farther from the surface of the body","deep",["pressure","nutrient","metabolism"],"deep: describes a position farther from the surface of the body.","The answer begins with D and has 1 word.","fs-id2104133","f-d339e6589f2d"],["q1-36831a7cecd9","s1.4-fc7f5552e1ab","1.4","Nutrients","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nchemical obtained from foods and beverages that is critical to human survival","nutrient",["metabolism","deep","pressure"],"nutrient: chemical obtained from foods and beverages that is critical to human survival.","The answer begins with N and has 1 word.","fs-id2532130","f-c2c3d576b21d"],["q1-715a011c1e53","s1.4-fc7f5552e1ab","1.4","Nutrients","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ is a substance in foods and beverages that is essential to human survival.","nutrient",["deep","pressure","metabolism"],"A nutrient is a substance in foods and beverages that is essential to human survival.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2532130","f-c18e606c31d4"],["q1-40cc09cf9b60","s1.4-3b4c0c6565e0","1.4","Narrow Range of Atmospheric Pressure","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAtmospheric ____ does more than just keep blood gases dissolved.","pressure",["deep","metabolism","nutrient"],"Atmospheric pressure does more than just keep blood gases dissolved.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1648023","f-e29a5ebe9dbb"],["q1-eddf832614a6","s1.4-3b4c0c6565e0","1.4","Narrow Range of Atmospheric Pressure","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nYour ability to breathe—that is, to take in oxygen and release carbon dioxide—also depends upon a precise atmospheric ____.","pressure",["deep","nutrient","metabolism"],"Your ability to breathe—that is, to take in oxygen and release carbon dioxide—also depends upon a precise atmospheric pressure.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1648023","f-76d97250baf6"],["q1-fa982f40eaae","s1.4-3b4c0c6565e0","1.4","Narrow Range of Atmospheric Pressure","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nMountain climbers carry oxygen to reduce the effects of both low oxygen levels and low barometric ____ at higher altitudes.","pressure",["metabolism","deep","nutrient"],"Mountain climbers carry oxygen to reduce the effects of both low oxygen levels and low barometric pressure at higher altitudes.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1648023","f-c990fa9bb2f1"],["q1-f303206cd4af","s1.4-3b4c0c6565e0","1.4","Decompression Sickness","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIn all cases, DCS is brought about by a reduction in barometric ____.","pressure",["nutrient","deep","metabolism"],"In all cases, DCS is brought about by a reduction in barometric pressure.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2153756","f-b7d386cc27ce"],["q1-269a2c54e25c","s1.4-3b4c0c6565e0","1.4","Decompression Sickness","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nNot surprisingly, diving in deep mountain lakes, where barometric ____ at the surface of the lake is less than that at sea level is more likely to result in DCS than diving in water at sea level.","pressure",["metabolism","nutrient","reproduction"],"Not surprisingly, diving in deep mountain lakes, where barometric pressure at the surface of the lake is less than that at sea level is more likely to result in DCS than diving in water at sea level.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2153756","f-1d75bc63f755"],["q1-9192b16ee30e","s1.4-3b4c0c6565e0","1.4","Decompression Sickness","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nWhen ____ is reduced, less gas remains dissolved.","pressure",["nutrient","deep","metabolism"],"When pressure is reduced, less gas remains dissolved.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1884492","f-cd1d912db866"],["q1-8b2df4743165","s1.4-3b4c0c6565e0","1.4","Decompression Sickness","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nRemoving the seal of the bottle reduces the ____ of the gas over the liquid.","pressure",["metabolism","deep","nutrient"],"Removing the seal of the bottle reduces the pressure of the gas over the liquid.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1884492","f-96318ce5feb6"],["q1-3219053d77ce","s1.4-3b4c0c6565e0","1.4","Decompression Sickness","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA hyperbaric chamber is a reinforced, closed chamber that is pressurized to greater than atmospheric ____.","pressure",["metabolism","nutrient","deep"],"A hyperbaric chamber is a reinforced, closed chamber that is pressurized to greater than atmospheric pressure.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2431360","f-47f57bce4dcd"],["q1-70bc7176c8a2","s1.5-5e78f1de16f3","1.5","Negative Feedback","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ncompares values to their normal range; deviations cause the activation of an effector","control center",["set point","negative feedback","positive feedback"],"control center: compares values to their normal range; deviations cause the activation of an effector.","The answer begins with C and has 2 words.","fs-id2239556","f-337d79b91e97"],["q1-34fc97ced965","s1.5-136e38a19407","1.5","Negative Feedback","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\norgan that can cause a change in a value","effector",["metabolism","homeostasis","physiology"],"effector: organ that can cause a change in a value.","The answer begins with E and has 1 word.","fs-id2239556","f-414e6bde04eb"],["q1-1b3d2000d043","s1.5-9f359d55968d","1.5","Homeostasis","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nhomeostatic mechanism that tends to stabilize an upset in the body’s physiological condition by preventing an excessive response to a stimulus, typically as the stimulus is removed","negative feedback",["normal range","control center","positive feedback"],"negative feedback: homeostatic mechanism that tends to stabilize an upset in the body’s physiological condition by preventing an excessive response to a stimulus, typically as the stimulus is removed.","The answer begins with N and has 2 words.","eip-991","f-960de2cff118"],["q1-4de967e4469a","s1.5-143b1b9af0bd","1.5","Homeostasis","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nrange of values around the set point that do not cause a reaction by the control center","normal range",["negative feedback","positive feedback","pressure"],"normal range: range of values around the set point that do not cause a reaction by the control center.","The answer begins with N and has 2 words.","eip-991","f-58aee4e8462b"],["q1-cbe2bacaad91","s1.5-23c7b3a0d5b4","1.5","Positive Feedback","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmechanism that intensifies a change in the body’s physiological condition in response to a stimulus","positive feedback",["normal range","set point","control center"],"positive feedback: mechanism that intensifies a change in the body’s physiological condition in response to a stimulus.","The answer begins with P and has 2 words.","fs-id1408923","f-7010b51ab66e"],["q1-8c787e966160","s1.5-2fc03be5f18a","1.5","Negative Feedback","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\n(also, receptor) reports a monitored physiological value to the control center","sensor",["pressure","physiology","effector"],"sensor: (also, receptor) reports a monitored physiological value to the control center.","The answer begins with S and has 1 word.","fs-id2239556","f-9da44170904f"],["q1-4e29914300c1","s1.5-bfcc9ba3f51a","1.5","Homeostasis","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nideal value for a physiological parameter; the level or small range within which a physiological parameter such as blood pressure is stable and optimally healthful, that is, within its parameters of homeostasis","set point",["negative feedback","normal range","positive feedback"],"set point: ideal value for a physiological parameter; the level or small range within which a physiological parameter such as blood pressure is stable and optimally healthful, that is, within its parameters of homeostasis.","The answer begins with S and has 2 words.","eip-991","f-ae9db726870b"],["q1-91e98f55e27d","s1.5-bfcc9ba3f51a","1.5","Homeostasis","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nFrom body temperature to blood pressure to levels of certain nutrients, each physiological condition has a particular ____.","set point",["normal range","positive feedback","control center"],"From body temperature to blood pressure to levels of certain nutrients, each physiological condition has a particular set point.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","eip-991","f-059d74aeae08"],["q1-a7eef36d21c3","s1.5-143b1b9af0bd","1.5","Homeostasis","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA set point is the physiological value around which the ____ fluctuates.","normal range",["negative feedback","positive feedback","control center"],"A set point is the physiological value around which the normal range fluctuates.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","eip-991","f-9b05d420f15b"],["q1-db78079da2f0","s1.5-143b1b9af0bd","1.5","Homeostasis","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ is the restricted set of values that is optimally healthful and stable.","normal range",["positive feedback","set point","negative feedback"],"A normal range is the restricted set of values that is optimally healthful and stable.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","eip-991","f-cd26f80496e5"],["q1-d5e8748eba19","s1.5-9f359d55968d","1.5","Homeostasis","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nControl centers in the brain and other parts of the body monitor and react to deviations from homeostasis using ____.","negative feedback",["positive feedback","normal range","control center"],"Control centers in the brain and other parts of the body monitor and react to deviations from homeostasis using negative feedback.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","eip-991","f-6c5d4516876f"],["q1-91abde88e330","s1.5-9f359d55968d","1.5","Homeostasis","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ is a mechanism that reverses a deviation from the set point.","negative feedback",["normal range","control center","positive feedback"],"Negative feedback is a mechanism that reverses a deviation from the set point.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","eip-991","f-583db3512013"],["q1-9f74132ec53b","s1.5-2fc03be5f18a","1.5","Negative Feedback","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____, also referred to a receptor, is a component of a feedback system that monitors a physiological value.","sensor",["deep","homeostasis","pressure"],"A sensor, also referred to a receptor, is a component of a feedback system that monitors a physiological value.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2239556","f-7541f33b7b72"],["q1-9e9ea7fd5dbf","s1.5-5e78f1de16f3","1.5","Negative Feedback","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ is the component in a feedback system that compares the value to the normal range.","control center",["positive feedback","set point","negative feedback"],"The control center is the component in a feedback system that compares the value to the normal range.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2239556","f-3d3f2c8621fc"],["q1-285d605d0c7b","s1.5-143b1b9af0bd","1.5","Negative Feedback","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAn effector is the component in a feedback system that causes a change to reverse the situation and return the value to the ____.","normal range",["set point","positive feedback","control center"],"An effector is the component in a feedback system that causes a change to reverse the situation and return the value to the normal range.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2239556","f-66f012c12f21"],["q1-7e01aa812e6d","s1.5-9f359d55968d","1.5","Negative Feedback","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAs glucose concentration in the bloodstream drops, the decrease in concentration—the actual ____—is detected by pancreatic alpha cells, and insulin release stops.","negative feedback",["set point","control center","normal range"],"As glucose concentration in the bloodstream drops, the decrease in concentration—the actual negative feedback—is detected by pancreatic alpha cells, and insulin release stops.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1291623","f-4acf2119361d"],["q1-055004c33e95","s1.5-3dde59ff3d79","1.5","Negative Feedback","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nActivation of the brain’s heat-gain center by exposure to cold reduces blood flow to the skin, and blood returning from the limbs is diverted into a network of ____ veins.","deep",["pressure","metabolism","homeostasis"],"In contrast, activation of the brain’s heat-gain center by exposure to cold reduces blood flow to the skin, and blood returning from the limbs is diverted into a network of deep veins.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2226448","f-0734054f2906"],["q1-46ff6a14cee4","s1.5-7020ffd45876","1.5","Negative Feedback","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe brain triggers the thyroid gland in the ____ to release thyroid hormone, which increases metabolic activity and heat production in cells throughout the body.","endocrine system",["set point","normal range","negative feedback"],"The brain triggers the thyroid gland in the endocrine system to release thyroid hormone, which increases metabolic activity and heat production in cells throughout the body.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2226448","f-52915fc83123"],["q1-f2f34e368665","s1.5-23c7b3a0d5b4","1.5","Positive Feedback","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ in the body is normal only when there is a definite end point.","positive feedback",["normal range","set point","negative feedback"],"Positive feedback in the body is normal only when there is a definite end point.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1408923","f-23a83b308f4c"],["q1-786c393569dc","s1.5-23c7b3a0d5b4","1.5","Positive Feedback","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nChildbirth and the body’s response to blood loss are two examples of ____ loops that are normal but are activated only when needed.","positive feedback",["normal range","control center","set point"],"Childbirth and the body’s response to blood loss are two examples of positive feedback loops that are normal but are activated only when needed.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1408923","f-1dbdd56a6a3f"],["q1-fec3ef66d0a2","s1.5-23c7b3a0d5b4","1.5","Positive Feedback","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe extreme muscular work of labor and delivery are the result of a ____ system.","positive feedback",["control center","negative feedback","normal range"],"The extreme muscular work of labor and delivery are the result of a positive feedback system.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2104151","f-43405a197024"],["q1-b7444270ef9b","s1.5-3b4c0c6565e0","1.5","Positive Feedback","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nLess blood circulating means reduced blood ____ and reduced perfusion (penetration of blood) to the brain and other vital organs.","pressure",["physiology","metabolism","deep"],"Less blood circulating means reduced blood pressure and reduced perfusion (penetration of blood) to the brain and other vital organs.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2239774","f-c89cf2c103c6"],["q1-7425235e3b00","s1.5-6e71092b156d","1.5","Positive Feedback","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIf ____ is severely reduced, vital organs will shut down and the person will die.","perfusion",["sensor","effector","deep"],"If perfusion is severely reduced, vital organs will shut down and the person will die.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2239774","f-2285ea7fe014"],["q1-cb41a3cbe97d","s1.6-cd75f1b4b455","1.6","Subdivisions of the Posterior (Dorsal) and Anterior (Ventral) Cavities","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndivision of the anterior (ventral) cavity that houses the abdominal and pelvic viscera","abdominopelvic cavity",["anatomical position","dorsal cavity","thoracic cavity"],"abdominopelvic cavity: division of the anterior (ventral) cavity that houses the abdominal and pelvic viscera.","The answer begins with A and has 2 words.","fs-id1289832","f-89e18c217f0d"],["q1-083d3c694e46","s1.6-63919c0b8fe2","1.6","Anatomical Position","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nstandard reference position used for describing locations and directions on the human body","anatomical position",["ventral cavity","oblique plane","anterior cavity"],"anatomical position: standard reference position used for describing locations and directions on the human body.","The answer begins with A and has 2 words.","fs-id2295576","f-1c7f9645130a"],["q1-c4a75962e310","s1.6-d863b2e34e4a","1.6","Anatomical Position","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndescribes the front or direction toward the front of the body; also referred to as ventral","anterior",["anatomy","medial","caudal"],"anterior: describes the front or direction toward the front of the body; also referred to as ventral.","The answer begins with A and has 1 word.","fs-id2295576","f-69794cc53f6a"],["q1-26eab4293d0b","s1.6-fd620c0822ee","1.6","Subdivisions of the Posterior (Dorsal) and Anterior (Ventral) Cavities","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nlarger body cavity located anterior to the posterior (dorsal) body cavity; includes the serous membrane-lined pleural cavities for the lungs, pericardial cavity for the heart, and peritoneal cavity for the abdominal and pelvic organs; also referred to as ventral cavity","anterior cavity",["anatomical position","dorsal cavity","abdominopelvic cavity"],"anterior cavity: larger body cavity located anterior to the posterior (dorsal) body cavity; includes the serous membrane-lined pleural cavities for the lungs, pericardial cavity for the heart, and peritoneal cavity for the abdominal and pelvic organs; also referred to as ventral cavity.","The answer begins with A and has 2 words.","fs-id1289832","f-7602ebb8a209"],["q1-2f51afa4f8cb","s1.6-c3f214314a69","1.6","Directional Terms","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndescribes a position below or lower than another part of the body proper; near or toward the tail (in humans, the coccyx, or lowest part of the spinal column); also referred to as inferior","caudal",["deep","pleura","distal"],"caudal: describes a position below or lower than another part of the body proper; near or toward the tail (in humans, the coccyx, or lowest part of the spinal column); also referred to as inferior.","The answer begins with C and has 1 word.","fs-id1898112","f-d7a91305328b"],["q1-b31ce574f065","s1.6-b45cf853be3b","1.6","Directional Terms","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndescribes a position above or higher than another part of the body proper; also referred to as superior","cranial",["pressure","anterior","caudal"],"cranial: describes a position above or higher than another part of the body proper; also referred to as superior.","The answer begins with C and has 1 word.","fs-id1898112","f-5d14a9e24abd"],["q1-7c2d45e1fcd2","s1.6-cda3b1da7246","1.6","Subdivisions of the Posterior (Dorsal) and Anterior (Ventral) Cavities","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndivision of the posterior (dorsal) cavity that houses the brain","cranial cavity",["thoracic cavity","transverse plane","oblique plane"],"cranial cavity: division of the posterior (dorsal) cavity that houses the brain.","The answer begins with C and has 2 words.","fs-id2761919","f-d95ca6aa629f"],["q1-1f2a3a381895","s1.6-ca8a2e83044d","1.6","Directional Terms","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndescribes a position farther from the point of attachment or the trunk of the body","distal",["pleura","peritoneum","cranial"],"distal: describes a position farther from the point of attachment or the trunk of the body.","The answer begins with D and has 1 word.","fs-id1898112","f-16a04d5103a7"],["q1-cfcbd0a670ef","s1.6-2483dda9da7d","1.6","Directional Terms","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndescribes the back or direction toward the back of the body; also referred to as posterior","dorsal",["cranial","superior","caudal"],"dorsal: describes the back or direction toward the back of the body; also referred to as posterior.","The answer begins with D and has 1 word.","fs-id1898112","f-1df55acbd2d4"],["q1-816e514001c2","s1.6-49ef90cf9466","1.6","Body Cavities and Serous Membranes","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nposterior body cavity that houses the brain and spinal cord; also referred to the posterior body cavity","dorsal cavity",["frontal plane","serous membrane","anterior cavity"],"dorsal cavity: posterior body cavity that houses the brain and spinal cord; also referred to the posterior body cavity.","The answer begins with D and has 2 words.","fig-ch01_06_04","f-1c164a66d346"],["q1-58d8b64b0594","s1.6-b35213aab472","1.6","Body Planes","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ntwo-dimensional, vertical plane that divides the body or organ into anterior and posterior portions","frontal plane",["abdominopelvic cavity","dorsal cavity","cranial cavity"],"frontal plane: two-dimensional, vertical plane that divides the body or organ into anterior and posterior portions.","The answer begins with F and has 2 words.","fs-id2025531","f-b6e0ee4ff06b"],["q1-25b512b965e7","s1.6-9278fe9049f0","1.6","Directional Terms","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndescribes a position below or lower than another part of the body proper; near or toward the tail (in humans, the coccyx, or lowest part of the spinal column); also referred to as caudal","inferior",["cranial","deep","posterior"],"inferior: describes a position below or lower than another part of the body proper; near or toward the tail (in humans, the coccyx, or lowest part of the spinal column); also referred to as caudal.","The answer begins with I and has 1 word.","fs-id2146154","f-a245bc6c4648"],["q1-f5ff62a499de","s1.6-913c620bbe83","1.6","Directional Terms","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndescribes the side or direction toward the side of the body","lateral",["proximal","reproduction","serosa"],"lateral: describes the side or direction toward the side of the body.","The answer begins with L and has 1 word.","fs-id1898112","f-5a61b6b95b9a"],["q1-7b8e3c737351","s1.6-c36e492e0590","1.6","Directional Terms","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndescribes the middle or direction toward the middle of the body","medial",["superficial","pressure","lateral"],"medial: describes the middle or direction toward the middle of the body.","The answer begins with M and has 1 word.","fs-id1898112","f-76ffc60f366a"],["q1-03f147d6e634","s1.6-87deea81474e","1.6","Body Planes","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ntwo-dimensional, vertical plane that intersects the body at an angle and is not parallel of perpendicular to any of the other three commonly used planes","oblique plane",["dorsal cavity","spinal cavity","anterior cavity"],"oblique plane: two-dimensional, vertical plane that intersects the body at an angle and is not parallel of perpendicular to any of the other three commonly used planes.","The answer begins with O and has 2 words.","fs-id2025531","f-784cdb7e9385"],["q1-f67810d8f725","s1.6-6fedbccbf6a0","1.6","Membranes of the Anterior (Ventral) Body Cavity","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nsac that encloses the heart","pericardium",["ventral","cranial","plane"],"pericardium: sac that encloses the heart.","The answer begins with P and has 1 word.","fs-id1465748","f-52c706a2a1f7"],["q1-2d5502848b2e","s1.6-8c0978527849","1.6","Membranes of the Anterior (Ventral) Body Cavity","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nserous membrane that lines the abdominopelvic cavity and covers the organs found there","peritoneum",["serosa","posterior","pressure"],"peritoneum: serous membrane that lines the abdominopelvic cavity and covers the organs found there.","The answer begins with P and has 1 word.","fs-id1465748","f-02a9830a093b"],["q1-5bbdddb54848","s1.6-2e869b90408c","1.6","Body Planes","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nimaginary two-dimensional surface that passes through the body","plane",["lateral","distal","reproduction"],"plane: imaginary two-dimensional surface that passes through the body.","The answer begins with P and has 1 word.","fs-id1530377","f-68adddc52d93"],["q1-930d70e18fac","s1.6-c75a90145876","1.6","Membranes of the Anterior (Ventral) Body Cavity","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nserous membrane that lines the pleural cavity and covers the lungs","pleura",["lateral","superior","reproduction"],"pleura: serous membrane that lines the pleural cavity and covers the lungs.","The answer begins with P and has 1 word.","fs-id1465748","f-0b651b2d64c2"],["q1-a3c6c4146de7","s1.6-0fba9ce50071","1.6","Anatomical Position","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndescribes the back or direction toward the back of the body; also referred to as dorsal","posterior",["peritoneum","supine","pleura"],"posterior: describes the back or direction toward the back of the body; also referred to as dorsal.","The answer begins with P and has 1 word.","fig-ch01_06_01","f-318fcf71fbcf"],["q1-260e2919e482","s1.6-e6fc1d9c31d6","1.6","Anatomical Position","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nface down","prone",["pleura","peritoneum","deep"],"prone: face down.","The answer begins with P and has 1 word.","fs-id2005951","f-72bc7058ff35"],["q1-5172502f3938","s1.6-ea497d9bac56","1.6","Directional Terms","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndescribes a position nearer to the point of attachment or the trunk of the body","proximal",["inferior","plane","lateral"],"proximal: describes a position nearer to the point of attachment or the trunk of the body.","The answer begins with P and has 1 word.","fs-id1898112","f-3c52a3c81404"],["q1-41d80794a9a7","s1.6-6941f62cf9c6","1.6","Body Planes","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ntwo-dimensional, vertical plane that divides the body or organ into right and left sides","sagittal plane",["frontal plane","anterior cavity","anatomical position"],"sagittal plane: two-dimensional, vertical plane that divides the body or organ into right and left sides.","The answer begins with S and has 2 words.","fs-id2025531","f-85b3d7ef35e5"],["q1-3621fd5184b2","s1.6-20182fbc1071","1.6","Body Planes","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nin anatomy, a single flat surface of a three-dimensional structure that has been cut through","section",["prone","lateral","plane"],"section: in anatomy, a single flat surface of a three-dimensional structure that has been cut through.","The answer begins with S and has 1 word.","fs-id1530377","f-48ecbcaf0a2e"],["q1-e891c1fc303f","s1.6-1e00f6804dc4","1.6","Membranes of the Anterior (Ventral) Body Cavity","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmembrane that covers organs and reduces friction; also referred to as serous membrane","serosa",["dorsal","deep","reproduction"],"serosa: membrane that covers organs and reduces friction; also referred to as serous membrane.","The answer begins with S and has 1 word.","fs-id1574091","f-4f89ae9f1475"],["q1-2a6a4a6d8abb","s1.6-2c04841707f5","1.6","Membranes of the Anterior (Ventral) Body Cavity","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmembrane that covers organs and reduces friction; also referred to as serosa","serous membrane",["anterior cavity","thoracic cavity","frontal plane"],"serous membrane: membrane that covers organs and reduces friction; also referred to as serosa.","The answer begins with S and has 2 words.","fs-id1574091","f-918b7f40c4cd"],["q1-77e71046225e","s1.6-98d28762fabc","1.6","Subdivisions of the Posterior (Dorsal) and Anterior (Ventral) Cavities","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndivision of the dorsal cavity that houses the spinal cord; also referred to as vertebral cavity","spinal cavity",["anatomical position","frontal plane","transverse plane"],"spinal cavity: division of the dorsal cavity that houses the spinal cord; also referred to as vertebral cavity.","The answer begins with S and has 2 words.","fs-id2761919","f-cc0d92a032d4"],["q1-eef0f9d2726f","s1.6-5a370c48ee47","1.6","Directional Terms","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndescribes a position nearer to the surface of the body","superficial",["peritoneum","anatomy","deep"],"superficial: describes a position nearer to the surface of the body.","The answer begins with S and has 1 word.","fs-id2146154","f-40daff42629a"],["q1-5813d990400f","s1.6-063dcf21f72d","1.6","Directional Terms","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndescribes a position above or higher than another part of the body proper; also referred to as cranial","superior",["plane","prone","lateral"],"superior: describes a position above or higher than another part of the body proper; also referred to as cranial.","The answer begins with S and has 1 word.","fs-id1898112","f-209e9f1860b5"],["q1-48078c5e1ba2","s1.6-7cc5f2b19879","1.6","Anatomical Position","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nface up","supine",["caudal","posterior","ventral"],"supine: face up.","The answer begins with S and has 1 word.","fs-id2005951","f-fb05a4cd33eb"],["q1-9e1685d793d0","s1.6-860ddc98ee7c","1.6","Subdivisions of the Posterior (Dorsal) and Anterior (Ventral) Cavities","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndivision of the anterior (ventral) cavity that houses the heart, lungs, esophagus, and trachea","thoracic cavity",["cranial cavity","sagittal plane","frontal plane"],"thoracic cavity: division of the anterior (ventral) cavity that houses the heart, lungs, esophagus, and trachea.","The answer begins with T and has 2 words.","fs-id1289832","f-6d2964e436ad"],["q1-316793df5a2e","s1.6-3227fb413703","1.6","Body Planes","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ntwo-dimensional, horizontal plane that divides the body or organ into superior and inferior portions","transverse plane",["sagittal plane","thoracic cavity","dorsal cavity"],"transverse plane: two-dimensional, horizontal plane that divides the body or organ into superior and inferior portions.","The answer begins with T and has 2 words.","fs-id2025531","f-66964a37b588"],["q1-eb7afbcafda0","s1.6-340718b76bba","1.6","Directional Terms","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ndescribes the front or direction toward the front of the body; also referred to as anterior","ventral",["lateral","deep","anatomy"],"ventral: describes the front or direction toward the front of the body; also referred to as anterior.","The answer begins with V and has 1 word.","fs-id1898112","f-bef1f70fd35c"],["q1-3d54915b7bbe","s1.6-e8b9b30956c6","1.6","Body Cavities and Serous Membranes","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nlarger body cavity located anterior to the posterior (dorsal) body cavity; includes the serous membrane-lined pleural cavities for the lungs, pericardial cavity for the heart, and peritoneal cavity for the abdominal and pelvic organs; also referred to as anterior body cavity","ventral cavity",["abdominopelvic cavity","sagittal plane","anterior cavity"],"ventral cavity: larger body cavity located anterior to the posterior (dorsal) body cavity; includes the serous membrane-lined pleural cavities for the lungs, pericardial cavity for the heart, and peritoneal cavity for the abdominal and pelvic organs; also referred to as anterior body cavity.","The answer begins with V and has 2 words.","fs-id1959778","f-fb4ada32cdb0"],["q1-94be3de72ad2","s1.6-63919c0b8fe2","1.6","Anatomical Position","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nJust as maps are normally oriented with north at the top, the standard body “map,” or ____, is that of the body standing upright, with the feet at shoulder width and parallel, toes forward.","anatomical position",["cranial cavity","transverse plane","anterior cavity"],"Just as maps are normally oriented with north at the top, the standard body “map,” or anatomical position, is that of the body standing upright, with the feet at shoulder width and parallel, toes forward.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2295576","f-2d8641700099"],["q1-5e09f330a2db","s1.6-d863b2e34e4a","1.6","Anatomical Position","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA scar in the ____ (front) carpal (wrist) region” would be present on the palm side of the wrist.","“anterior",["prone","anatomy","cranial"],"For example, a scar in the “anterior (front) carpal (wrist) region” would be present on the palm side of the wrist.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2295576","f-65e66d255fe5"],["q1-a855ac7a0a0f","s1.6-d863b2e34e4a","1.6","Anatomical Position","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe term “____” would be used even if the hand were palm down on a table.","anterior",["supine","distal","anatomy"],"The term “anterior” would be used even if the hand were palm down on a table.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2295576","f-24663140b1ab"],["q1-1812c6a2e5c7","s1.6-7cc5f2b19879","1.6","Anatomical Position","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA body that is lying down is described as either prone or ____.","supine",["inferior","superficial","pericardium"],"A body that is lying down is described as either prone or supine.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2005951","f-a2d796b11ff7"],["q1-c96ac976780b","s1.6-7cc5f2b19879","1.6","Anatomical Position","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nProne describes a face-down orientation, and ____ describes a face up orientation.","supine",["lateral","posterior","pleura"],"Prone describes a face-down orientation, and supine describes a face up orientation.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2005951","f-dd9b9e975c29"],["q1-88886d9e4901","s1.6-d863b2e34e4a","1.6","Directional Terms","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ (or ventral ) Describes the front or direction toward the front of the body.","Anterior",["inferior","plane","posterior"],"Anterior (or ventral ) Describes the front or direction toward the front of the body.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1898112","f-148345b29ba9"],["q1-f1d61f7453c6","s1.6-0fba9ce50071","1.6","Directional Terms","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ (or dorsal ) Describes the back or direction toward the back of the body.","Posterior",["distal","serosa","peritoneum"],"Posterior (or dorsal ) Describes the back or direction toward the back of the body.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1898112","f-1148d8bc09e5"],["q1-265a92263766","s1.6-913c620bbe83","1.6","Directional Terms","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ describes the side or direction toward the side of the body.","Lateral",["pleura","supine","superior"],"Lateral describes the side or direction toward the side of the body.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1898112","f-5a61b6b95b9a"],["q1-c03c287fc705","s1.6-913c620bbe83","1.6","Directional Terms","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe thumb (pollex) is ____ to the digits.","lateral",["pleura","pressure","plane"],"The thumb (pollex) is lateral to the digits.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1898112","f-bcb3188eb511"],["q1-84d27195fb7f","s1.6-c36e492e0590","1.6","Directional Terms","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ describes the middle or direction toward the middle of the body.","Medial",["prone","caudal","serosa"],"Medial describes the middle or direction toward the middle of the body.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1898112","f-76ffc60f366a"],["q1-575a7f9ce201","s1.6-ea497d9bac56","1.6","Directional Terms","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ describes a position in a limb that is nearer to the point of attachment or the trunk of the body.","Proximal",["serosa","posterior","cranial"],"Proximal describes a position in a limb that is nearer to the point of attachment or the trunk of the body.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1898112","f-09b58f74e073"],["q1-dddb75c82c13","s1.6-ca8a2e83044d","1.6","Directional Terms","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ describes a position in a limb that is farther from the point of attachment or the trunk of the body.","Distal",["cranial","anatomy","pressure"],"Distal describes a position in a limb that is farther from the point of attachment or the trunk of the body.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1898112","f-199bc4092c77"],["q1-871cf5405cb1","s1.6-5a370c48ee47","1.6","Directional Terms","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ describes a position closer to the surface of the body.","Superficial",["superior","pleura","dorsal"],"Superficial describes a position closer to the surface of the body.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1898112","f-cc148b7ab36b"],["q1-6c784103641c","s1.6-3dde59ff3d79","1.6","Directional Terms","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ describes a position farther from the surface of the body.","Deep",["dorsal","reproduction","pleura"],"Deep describes a position farther from the surface of the body.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1898112","f-d339e6589f2d"],["q1-68b1c834cb94","s1.6-2e869b90408c","1.6","Body Planes","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ is an imaginary two-dimensional surface that passes through the body.","plane",["pericardium","lateral","supine"],"A plane is an imaginary two-dimensional surface that passes through the body.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1530377","f-c53b4c1c8160"],["q1-aafa2222d866","s1.6-6941f62cf9c6","1.6","Body Planes","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ is the plane that divides the body or an organ vertically into right and left sides.","sagittal plane",["spinal cavity","dorsal cavity","thoracic cavity"],"The sagittal plane is the plane that divides the body or an organ vertically into right and left sides.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2025531","f-d63607369def"],["q1-f098cb9167fe","s1.6-b35213aab472","1.6","Body Planes","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ is the plane that divides the body or an organ into an anterior (front) portion and a posterior (rear) portion.","frontal plane",["spinal cavity","transverse plane","serous membrane"],"The frontal plane is the plane that divides the body or an organ into an anterior (front) portion and a posterior (rear) portion.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2025531","f-88641e3a541c"],["q1-197bac7504f3","s1.6-b35213aab472","1.6","Body Planes","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ is often referred to as a coronal plane.","frontal plane",["abdominopelvic cavity","dorsal cavity","anterior cavity"],"The frontal plane is often referred to as a coronal plane.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2025531","f-0ad880bdf8f4"],["q1-b0bba80e20bc","s1.6-3227fb413703","1.6","Body Planes","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ is the plane that divides the body or organ horizontally into upper and lower portions.","transverse plane",["thoracic cavity","abdominopelvic cavity","oblique plane"],"The transverse plane is the plane that divides the body or organ horizontally into upper and lower portions.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2025531","f-dfe3ae1dea68"],["q1-c7541628b5ac","s1.6-60d5a1457645","1.6","Body Cavities and Serous Membranes","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ and the ventral (anterior) cavity are the largest body compartments.","dorsal (posterior) cavity",["oblique plane","frontal plane","spinal cavity"],"The dorsal (posterior) cavity and the ventral (anterior) cavity are the largest body compartments.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1959778","f-edb097846323"],["q1-b63ab267391d","s1.6-0fba9ce50071","1.6","Subdivisions of the Posterior (Dorsal) and Anterior (Ventral) Cavities","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ (dorsal) and anterior (ventral) cavities are each subdivided into smaller cavities.","posterior",["pleura","pressure","pericardium"],"The posterior (dorsal) and anterior (ventral) cavities are each subdivided into smaller cavities.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2761919","f-918f4a5467f1"],["q1-1b37a02bc3e3","s1.6-cda3b1da7246","1.6","Subdivisions of the Posterior (Dorsal) and Anterior (Ventral) Cavities","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIn the posterior (dorsal) cavity, the ____ houses the brain, and the spinal cavity (or vertebral cavity) encloses the spinal cord.","cranial cavity",["ventral cavity","thoracic cavity","sagittal plane"],"In the posterior (dorsal) cavity, the cranial cavity houses the brain, and the spinal cavity (or vertebral cavity) encloses the spinal cord.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2761919","f-6831a5d8efbc"],["q1-c387f4a9e7f6","s1.6-0fba9ce50071","1.6","Subdivisions of the Posterior (Dorsal) and Anterior (Ventral) Cavities","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe brain and spinal cord are protected by the bones of the skull and vertebral column and by cerebrospinal fluid, a colorless fluid produced by the brain, which cushions the brain and spinal cord within the ____ (dorsal) cavity.","posterior",["pericardium","cranial","medial"],"The brain and spinal cord are protected by the bones of the skull and vertebral column and by cerebrospinal fluid, a colorless fluid produced by the brain, which cushions the brain and spinal cord within the posterior (dorsal) cavity.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2761919","f-b4a124565b8f"],["q1-00b2ed70bcee","s1.6-cd75f1b4b455","1.6","Subdivisions of the Posterior (Dorsal) and Anterior (Ventral) Cavities","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe anterior (ventral) cavity has two main subdivisions: the thoracic cavity and the ____.","abdominopelvic cavity",["oblique plane","transverse plane","spinal cavity"],"The anterior (ventral) cavity has two main subdivisions: the thoracic cavity and the abdominopelvic cavity.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1289832","f-b1ef22268d2d"],["q1-3619d901cbc0","s1.6-860ddc98ee7c","1.6","Subdivisions of the Posterior (Dorsal) and Anterior (Ventral) Cavities","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ contains the lungs and the heart, which is located in the mediastinum.","thoracic cavity",["sagittal plane","frontal plane","anterior cavity"],"The thoracic cavity contains the lungs and the heart, which is located in the mediastinum.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1289832","f-c8bef5a26822"],["q1-335e03d31590","s1.6-cd75f1b4b455","1.6","Subdivisions of the Posterior (Dorsal) and Anterior (Ventral) Cavities","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ is the largest cavity in the body.","abdominopelvic cavity",["ventral cavity","transverse plane","dorsal cavity"],"The abdominopelvic cavity is the largest cavity in the body.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1289832","f-25197930548c"],["q1-1ce1e6914fde","s1.6-063dcf21f72d","1.6","Abdominal Regions and Quadrants","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe more detailed regional approach subdivides the cavity with one horizontal line immediately inferior to the ribs and one immediately ____ to the pelvis, and two vertical lines drawn as if dropped from the midpoint of each clavicle (collarbone).","superior",["serosa","cranial","prone"],"The more detailed regional approach subdivides the cavity with one horizontal line immediately inferior to the ribs and one immediately superior to the pelvis, and two vertical lines drawn as if dropped from the midpoint of each clavicle (collarbone).","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1927339","f-eca2a40837cf"],["q1-e88c0c30be05","s1.6-2c04841707f5","1.6","Membranes of the Anterior (Ventral) Body Cavity","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ (also referred to a serosa) is one of the thin membranes that cover the walls and organs in the thoracic and abdominopelvic cavities.","serous membrane",["transverse plane","frontal plane","anterior cavity"],"A serous membrane (also referred to a serosa) is one of the thin membranes that cover the walls and organs in the thoracic and abdominopelvic cavities.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1574091","f-38b105c74ac8"],["q1-d27076959cce","s1.6-2c04841707f5","1.6","Membranes of the Anterior (Ventral) Body Cavity","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe pleura is the ____ that encloses the pleural cavity; the pleural cavity surrounds the lungs.","serous membrane",["transverse plane","sagittal plane","oblique plane"],"The pleura is the serous membrane that encloses the pleural cavity; the pleural cavity surrounds the lungs.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1465748","f-7ac4b742379b"],["q1-638a758dd6e7","s1.6-2c04841707f5","1.6","Membranes of the Anterior (Ventral) Body Cavity","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe pericardium is the ____ that encloses the pericardial cavity; the pericardial cavity surrounds the heart.","serous membrane",["oblique plane","thoracic cavity","spinal cavity"],"The pericardium is the serous membrane that encloses the pericardial cavity; the pericardial cavity surrounds the heart.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1465748","f-2ec677d8a5d9"],["q1-d1e00ce3beaa","s1.6-cd75f1b4b455","1.6","Membranes of the Anterior (Ventral) Body Cavity","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe peritoneum is the serous membrane that encloses the peritoneal cavity; the peritoneal cavity surrounds several organs in the ____.","abdominopelvic cavity",["thoracic cavity","dorsal cavity","sagittal plane"],"The peritoneum is the serous membrane that encloses the peritoneal cavity; the peritoneal cavity surrounds several organs in the abdominopelvic cavity.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1465748","f-52ccd2de5a43"],["q1-272cbb54ef1c","s1.6-1e00f6804dc4","1.6","Membranes of the Anterior (Ventral) Body Cavity","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nBoth the parietal and visceral ____ secrete the thin, slippery serous fluid located within the serous cavities.","serosa",["anterior","posterior","lateral"],"Both the parietal and visceral serosa secrete the thin, slippery serous fluid located within the serous cavities.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1465748","f-e259d4bbed36"],["q1-962b9987a1ea","s1.6-6fedbccbf6a0","1.6","Membranes of the Anterior (Ventral) Body Cavity","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe pericardial cavity reduces friction between the heart and the wall of the ____.","pericardium",["pressure","medial","plane"],"Likewise, the pericardial cavity reduces friction between the heart and the wall of the pericardium.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1465748","f-4064b235db05"],["q1-1deddceff3eb","s1.7-fce6059425c9","1.7","Computed Tomography","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmedical imaging technique in which a computer-enhanced cross-sectional X-ray image is obtained","computed tomography (CT)",["metabolism","magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)","growth"],"computed tomography (CT): medical imaging technique in which a computer-enhanced cross-sectional X-ray image is obtained.","The answer begins with C and has 3 words.","fs-id2569694","f-d9f10a2e4109"],["q1-8fed1d56d7f1","s1.7-e1d80e756fa0","1.7","Magnetic Resonance Imaging","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmedical imaging technique in which a device generates a magnetic field to obtain detailed sectional images of the internal structures of the body","magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)",["positron emission tomography (PET)","computed tomography (CT)","spinal cavity"],"magnetic resonance imaging (MRI): medical imaging technique in which a device generates a magnetic field to obtain detailed sectional images of the internal structures of the body.","The answer begins with M and has 4 words.","fs-id2523214","f-c2a7d4b56c85"],["q1-2d81077cc532","s1.7-a936b2aee421","1.7","Positron Emission Tomography","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmedical imaging technique in which radiopharmaceuticals are traced to reveal metabolic and physiological functions in tissues","positron emission tomography (PET)",["magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)","computed tomography (CT)","set point"],"positron emission tomography (PET): medical imaging technique in which radiopharmaceuticals are traced to reveal metabolic and physiological functions in tissues.","The answer begins with P and has 4 words.","fs-id2299812","f-ec8ce0938a06"],["q1-95e2d263c42e","s1.7-64a035914d50","1.7","Ultrasonography","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\napplication of ultrasonic waves to visualize subcutaneous body structures such as tendons and organs","ultrasonography",["physiology","X-ray","growth"],"ultrasonography: application of ultrasonic waves to visualize subcutaneous body structures such as tendons and organs.","The answer begins with U and has 1 word.","fs-id1277947","f-d67f2e9f67a1"],["q1-4100f578ae8d","s1.7-4b4de01d7d49","1.7","X-Rays","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nform of high energy electromagnetic radiation with a short wavelength capable of penetrating solids and ionizing gases; used in medicine as a diagnostic aid to visualize body structures such as bones","X-ray",["ultrasonography","metabolism","anatomy"],"X-ray: form of high energy electromagnetic radiation with a short wavelength capable of penetrating solids and ionizing gases; used in medicine as a diagnostic aid to visualize body structures such as bones.","The answer begins with X and has 1 word.","fs-id2162168","f-409c7d95ac97"],["q1-152fefde400f","s1.7-a6a5b795304c","1.7","Medical Imaging","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nMedical schools began to teach ____ using human dissection; although some resorted to grave robbing to obtain corpses.","anatomy",["X-ray","nutrient","metabolism"],"Medical schools began to teach anatomy using human dissection; although some resorted to grave robbing to obtain corpses.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2614479","f-03bcc899f8bb"],["q1-93b24bd1092b","s1.7-4b4de01d7d49","1.7","X-Rays","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ is a form of high energy electromagnetic radiation with a short wavelength capable of penetrating solids and ionizing gases.","X-ray",["metabolism","growth","ultrasonography"],"The X-ray is a form of high energy electromagnetic radiation with a short wavelength capable of penetrating solids and ionizing gases.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1431659","f-2d2c347fc383"],["q1-1bee64824762","s1.7-4b4de01d7d49","1.7","X-Rays","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe beam of radiation results in darkening of the ____ plate.","X-ray",["growth","nutrient","physiology"],"The beam of radiation results in darkening of the X-ray plate.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1431659","f-b5ec0aca6c49"],["q1-5f30b5f4d2d7","s1.7-4b4de01d7d49","1.7","X-Rays","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nRefinements and enhancements of ____ techniques have continued throughout the twentieth and twenty-first centuries.","X-ray",["metabolism","physiology","ultrasonography"],"Refinements and enhancements of X-ray techniques have continued throughout the twentieth and twenty-first centuries.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2566590","f-d9e631d3eb41"],["q1-bc4c63de91dc","s1.7-4b4de01d7d49","1.7","X-Rays","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAlthough often supplanted by more sophisticated imaging techniques, the ____ remains a “workhorse” in medical imaging, especially for viewing fractures and for dentistry.","X-ray",["anatomy","ultrasonography","metabolism"],"Although often supplanted by more sophisticated imaging techniques, the X-ray remains a “workhorse” in medical imaging, especially for viewing fractures and for dentistry.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2566590","f-d1ad0c4f82e5"],["q1-2c92f4319c7e","s1.7-4b4de01d7d49","1.7","Computed Tomography","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIn the technique, a patient lies on a motorized platform while a computerized axial tomography (CAT) scanner rotates 360 degrees around the patient, taking ____ images.","X-ray",["anatomy","ultrasonography","metabolism"],"In the technique, a patient lies on a motorized platform while a computerized axial tomography (CAT) scanner rotates 360 degrees around the patient, taking X-ray images.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2569694","f-a6756bcda777"],["q1-fcb0fcdf1c7e","s1.7-e1d80e756fa0","1.7","Magnetic Resonance Imaging","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ is a noninvasive medical imaging technique based on a phenomenon of nuclear physics discovered in the 1930s, in which matter exposed to magnetic fields and radio waves was found to emit radio signals.","magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)",["positron emission tomography (PET)","computed tomography (CT)","sagittal plane"],"Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is a noninvasive medical imaging technique based on a phenomenon of nuclear physics discovered in the 1930s, in which matter exposed to magnetic fields and radio waves was found to emit radio signals.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2523214","f-958f6c935601"],["q1-d9a0ea0d4b44","s1.7-a936b2aee421","1.7","Positron Emission Tomography","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ is a medical imaging technique involving the use of so-called radiopharmaceuticals, substances that emit radiation that is short-lived and therefore relatively safe to administer to the body.","positron emission tomography (PET)",["magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)","computed tomography (CT)","negative feedback"],"Positron emission tomography (PET) is a medical imaging technique involving the use of so-called radiopharmaceuticals, substances that emit radiation that is short-lived and therefore relatively safe to administer to the body.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2299812","f-9a25692f9ad4"],["q1-b986085bfc29","s1.7-671e483513b1","1.7","Positron Emission Tomography","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe main advantage is that PET (see c ) can illustrate physiologic activity—including nutrient ____ and blood flow—of the organ or organs being targeted, whereas CT and MRI scans can only show static images.","metabolism",["X-ray","ultrasonography","anatomy"],"The main advantage is that PET (see c ) can illustrate physiologic activity—including nutrient metabolism and blood flow—of the organ or organs being targeted, whereas CT and MRI scans can only show static images.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2299812","f-b83e9124ff6a"],["q1-edit-962e198d6df9","s1.3-edit-7a33c06149c9","1.3","Organization","connections","What separates the fluid inside a cell from the fluid outside it?","The plasma membrane",["The nucleus alone","The extracellular matrix alone","The ribosome"],"The plasma membrane maintains a boundary between intracellular and extracellular environments.","Use the role or mechanism described in the question.","fs-id1371870","f-12c55b5aeb47"],["q1-edit-02e27c5154f7","s1.3-edit-84b8742b8918","1.3","Movement","connections","Which example shows movement inside the body without moving a joint?","Blood flowing through vessels",["Bone mineral remaining in place","An unchanged skin pigment","A resting tooth remaining anchored"],"Movement includes internal transport, organ motion, and cellular activity as well as movement at joints.","Use the role or mechanism described in the question.","fs-id1960688","f-851741b3bb86"],["q1-edit-3dca3ec22990","s1.4-edit-d47d78c8b6ca","1.4","Requirements for Human Life","connections","Why does survival require suitable temperature and pressure as well as nutrients and oxygen?","Physiological processes operate within limited physical conditions",["Cells function identically at every temperature and pressure","Nutrients replace the need for all physical conditions","Pressure controls only hair growth"],"Temperature and pressure help establish the conditions needed for normal physiological processes.","Use the role or mechanism described in the question.","fs-id1520530","f-eabfd786c788"],["q1-edit-123c63b68479","s1.4-edit-4f74302e4f2a","1.4","Oxygen","connections","Why are brain cells especially sensitive to an interruption of oxygen supply?","They require a steady production of ATP",["They store unlimited oxygen for future use","They make ATP only from bone minerals","They cannot use any chemical energy"],"Oxygen supports ATP production, and brain cells have a high, continuous energy requirement.","Use the role or mechanism described in the question.","fs-id2396763","f-baddd5ba1910"],["q1-edit-5d86ce4440b8","s1.4-edit-17265d431758","1.4","Narrow Range of Temperature","connections","How can an extreme change in body temperature disrupt metabolism?","It can alter enzyme structure and function",["It converts every enzyme into a carbohydrate","It makes enzyme activity independent of structure","It removes the need for ATP"],"Metabolic reactions depend on enzymes whose normal structure and function require suitable temperatures.","Use the role or mechanism described in the question.","fs-id1758474","f-1d10380f1d25"],["q1-edit-8d5d109d4f58","s1.4-edit-e7b2fb829e25","1.4","Controlled Hypothermia","connections","What physiological effect is the basis of controlled hypothermia discussed in this section?","A reduction in metabolic demand",["An increase in all metabolic reactions","Permanent elimination of the need for oxygen","Conversion of neurons into muscle cells"],"Lowering temperature can reduce metabolic rate and the metabolic demands of organs.","Use the role or mechanism described in the question.","fs-id1902785","f-669d45e95d60"],["q1-edit-4cebe0235071","s1.6-edit-48fdc55eb036","1.6","Anatomical Terminology","connections","Why are standardized anatomical terms useful?","They describe locations more precisely and reduce ambiguity",["They change meaning for each observer","They eliminate the need to identify any body region","They describe only diseases and never structures"],"Standard terms give observers a shared, precise way to describe structures and locations.","Use the role or mechanism described in the question.","fs-id2473278","f-b7ebe7a5f28a"],["q1-edit-e891c94c73a3","s1.6-edit-1079b99f2418","1.6","Regional Terms","connections","In anatomical regional terminology, what does the leg or crural region refer to?","The region between the knee and ankle",["The entire lower limb including the thigh","The region between the shoulder and elbow","Only the sole of the foot"],"The anatomical leg is the lower-limb region between the knee and ankle; the thigh is a separate region.","Use the role or mechanism described in the question.","fs-id2200372","f-c6fb7172155d"],["q1-edit-4b2222a2abbc","s1.7-edit-4c7fc1d84d2d","1.7","Modern Medical Imaging","connections","What distinguishes many modern imaging methods from a conventional single-angle X-ray?","Computer processing can provide sectional or three-dimensional information",["All modern imaging methods require an incision","They show only the outer skin surface","They cannot examine internal structures"],"Modern imaging uses computer-integrated data to show sections, three-dimensional structure, or aspects of function.","Use the role or mechanism described in the question.","fs-id2694909","f-1623ba070bbf"]]);
